---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import { ADAPTER_ROWS, PLANNED_AGENTS, STATUS_LABEL } from "../../components/docs/agents";
import AgentLogo from "../../components/AgentLogo.astro";

const proxy = `coding.sh init --proxy              # Claude Code and Codex
eval "$(coding.sh proxy env)"        # any other client`;
const hook = `/bin/sh "/Users/you/.coding.sh/bin/hook.sh" <agent-id> <EventName>   # absolute, quoted path
node "C:/Users/you/.coding.sh/bin/hook.mjs" <agent-id> <EventName>      # on Windows`;
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    What coding.sh captures from each agent, and how. Each agent has an adapter that installs capture, maps the agent's events into the
    <a href="/docs/trace-format">trace format</a>, imports past sessions and drives the agent headless for eval runs, in an isolated config so
    your own setup is never touched. Agents without hooks are recorded by wrapping the command: <code>coding.sh run -- &lt;cmd&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="matrix">Matrix</h2>
  <div class="doc-table doc-table--stack">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Agent</th>
          <th>Status</th>
          <th>Live capture</th>
          <th>Backfill</th>
          <th>Headless runs</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          ADAPTER_ROWS.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <AgentLogo id={a.id} name={a.name} size={18} class="agent-cell" />
                <br />
                <code class="t-muted">{a.id}</code>
              </td>
              <td data-label="Status">{STATUS_LABEL[a.status]}</td>
              <td data-label="Live capture">{a.live}</td>
              <td data-label="Backfill">{a.backfill ?? "None"}</td>
              <td data-label="Headless runs">
                <code>{a.headless}</code>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Verified on real sessions</td><td>Checked against real sessions of the agent.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Verified on a real install</td><td>The current release ran a session against a local stub model; its hooks fired, the session was checked, and the payloads are CI fixtures.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fixture-tested</td><td>CI checks it against the agent's documented and recorded formats. Nobody has run it on a live install yet.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    If you use a fixture-tested agent, an <a href="https://github.com/jackulau/coding.sh/issues/new?template=agent-support.yml">agent support report</a>
    saying whether it worked moves it to verified.
  </p>

  <h2 id="subagents">Subagents</h2>
  <p>
    A subagent appears as <code>subagent.start</code> and <code>subagent.end</code> events in the parent session, paired by
    <code>subagentId</code>. When the agent keeps a separate transcript or session for the subagent, that becomes a child session whose
    <code>parent</code> points back. <code>coding.sh sessions --subagents</code> lists child sessions.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Agent</th>
          <th>How subagents are captured</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          ADAPTER_ROWS.filter((a) => a.id !== "wrapper").map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="nowrap"><AgentLogo id={a.id} name={a.name} size={18} class="agent-cell" /></td>
              <td>{a.subagents}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="hook">The hook command</h2>
  <p>Every adapter installs the same command:</p>
  <Code code={hook} />
  <ul>
    <li>It exits 0 every time and prints nothing, unless an agent's protocol requires a reply (Cursor's <code>beforeSubmitPrompt</code> gets <code>&#123;"continue":true&#125;</code>).</li>
    <li>It never answers a permission question. Where an agent treats a hook's silence or exit code as a decision (Cursor's permission events, Copilot's <code>preToolUse</code>, Antigravity's <code>PreToolUse</code>, Amp's <code>tool.call</code>), that event is not registered at all.</li>
    <li>Entries are marked (<code>"_codingsh": true</code> where the format allows) so <code>uninstall</code> removes only ours. Where a format has no room for a marker, ownership is recorded in <code>~/.coding.sh/installs.json</code>.</li>
    <li>The original file is backed up to <code>~/.coding.sh/backups</code> before every edit.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="notes">Agent notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Codex</strong> runs new hooks only after you trust them with <code>/hooks</code>. coding.sh never writes trust hashes; eval runs pass <code>--dangerously-bypass-hook-trust</code> inside an isolated <code>CODEX_HOME</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Cursor</strong> also runs Claude Code hooks from <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. Those payloads are dropped by the Claude Code adapter, so each Cursor event is captured once.</li>
    <li><strong>Amp</strong> keeps threads server-side, so there is no local backfill.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kiro CLI</strong> has no adapter yet. Wrap it:
      <code>coding.sh run -- kiro-cli chat --v3 --no-interactive --trust-all-tools "…"</code>. That records start, end, exit code,
      duration and file changes, but not prompts, tool calls or tokens.
    </li>
    <li>
      Kiro's OpenTelemetry export is a daily push of account totals, not per-session spans, so the local OTLP receiver can't use it. The
      Kiro IDE is not captured.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Antigravity CLI</strong> (<code>agy</code>) replaced Gemini CLI. Record it with the <code>antigravity</code> adapter; <code>gemini-cli</code> still reads sessions from older installs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="proxy">Model API proxy</h2>
  <p>
    Hooks and transcripts record what an agent did. To also record what it sent the model (the full system prompt, tool definitions
    and messages of every call), route it through the local proxy:
  </p>
  <Code code={proxy} />
  <p>
    <code>init --proxy</code> points Claude Code and Codex at <code>coding.sh proxy</code> on <code>127.0.0.1:4320</code> and starts it. Each
    call lands in the session the hooks capture. An agent that already calls a gateway keeps calling it, through the proxy. See
    <a href="/docs/how-to/capture-with-proxy">Capture with the proxy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="other-agents">Other agents</h2>
  <p>{PLANNED_AGENTS.join(", ")} have no adapter yet. Record them with <code>coding.sh run -- &lt;cmd&gt;</code>, which keeps the start, end, exit code, duration and file changes.</p>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  :global(.agent-cell) {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  :global(.agent-cell .alogo__mark) {
    color: var(--text-2);
  }
</style>
